---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Kicker from '../components/content/Kicker.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/convert/CtaBand.astro';

const title = 'The PainToBuild Validation Methodology';
const description = 'How PainToBuild counts an independent signal, separates pain from willingness to pay, and decides build, change, watch or stop, worked through one real example.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PainToBuild' },
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`${title} | PainToBuild`} description={description} ogImagePath="/open-graph/methodology.png">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />

  <section class="wrap section prose">
    <Kicker>Methodology</Kicker>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every analysis on this site is built the same way, from the same rules. This page is that
      method in full, so any claim elsewhere on the site can be checked against the standard that
      produced it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="evidence-categories">What counts as evidence</h2>
    <p>
      A first-person account of a real problem, in the person's own words, from a public forum:
      Reddit posts and comments, Hacker News threads, or IndieHackers posts. Nothing here is
      survey data or a controlled study. It's public conversation, read carefully and counted
      strictly.
    </p>

    <h2 id="independence">What makes a signal independent</h2>
    <p>
      An independent signal is the same problem described by a <strong>different person</strong>,
      in their own first-person words. A different comment, or a repost of the same account, does
      not count. Specifically:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The same person posting more than once, anywhere, still counts as one.</li>
      <li>A cross-post of the same thread to another subreddit is deduplicated to one.</li>
      <li>An <strong>advisor</strong> answering in someone else's thread doesn't count. Only the
        original poster's own words do, and only if recovered and genuine.</li>
      <li>A <strong>meta observation</strong> ("I keep seeing people ask this") counts as
        corroboration, never as an independent signal on its own.</li>
      <li>A <strong>promotional post</strong> using the pain as a pitch hook for the poster's own
        product doesn't count as evidence of demand.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Threshold:</strong> below three independent signals, a candidate stays parked and no
      page gets built on it. At three or more, deeper validation starts.
    </p>

    <h2 id="strength-levels">Clean signals vs. weak signals</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>clean</strong> signal is a confirmed, first-person, non-casual account of recurring
      pain. A <strong>weak</strong> signal is real but thinner: a shorter post, a single cross-post
      chain, or language closer to curiosity than desperation. Every analysis states both counts
      separately instead of folding weak signals into the headline number.
    </p>

    <h2 id="confidence-tiers">Confidence tiers</h2>
    <p>
      Every published analysis carries one of two tags:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pain Validated:</strong> three or more independent, clean, first-person signals
        confirming a recurring problem.</li>
      <li><strong>Early Signal:</strong> real evidence exists but hasn't yet cleared the
        independent-signal bar.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Neither tag means "proven demand." <strong>Pain Validated means the pain is real and
      recurring. It does not mean anyone will pay to have it solved.</strong> That's a separate
      question, addressed below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contradictions">Contradictions and counter-evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Evidence that cuts against a candidate is logged with the same rigor as evidence for it.
      Most often that's a saturation signal: a competing product, a free tool, or a market
      observation that narrows the gap. A candidate can be pain-validated and still be a weak
      opportunity if counter-evidence shows the space is already well served.
    </p>

    <h2 id="external-validation">External / money-moving validation</h2>
    <p>
      Where possible, each analysis also checks for money already moving nearby: existing paid
      products, a stated price someone would pay, or a workaround someone paid for. Money-moving
      evidence is stronger than stated pain alone, because it shows behavior, not just words.
    </p>

    <h2 id="exclusions">What gets excluded</h2>
    <p>
      Advisor comments, meta commentary, self-promotional posts, and anything that can't be traced
      to a specific first-person sufferer are excluded from every signal count. They're logged when
      relevant, but never counted toward validation.
    </p>

    <h2 id="problem-vs-payment">Recurring pain is not proof of payment</h2>
    <p>
      This is the single most important distinction in the method. Someone describing a problem,
      even desperately, has not shown they would pay to have it solved. Every analysis on this site
      keeps those two claims separate.
    </p>

    <h2 id="limitations">Limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The sample is whoever posts publicly on Reddit, Hacker News and IndieHackers. It is not a
        representative survey of founders or of any wider population.</li>
      <li>This is one operator's reading of public text. It has not been through peer review.</li>
      <li>Evidence windows are dated and can go stale; each analysis states the window it covers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      No platform username is ever printed on this site. Every quote is attributed by platform and
      date only. This is a standing rule: a violation of it blocks a page from launch.
    </p>

    <h2 id="decision-criteria">Decision criteria</h2>
    <p>
      Once a candidate clears the independent-signal gate, it's scored across twelve dimensions,
      1–5 each: pain intensity, frequency, urgency, failed alternatives, willingness to pay,
      specificity, reachable audience, evidence strength, saturation gap, risk (reverse-scored,
      5 = safe), fast-cash feasibility, and long-term revenue potential. The composite score is
      the plain, unweighted mean of those twelve numbers, with no hidden weighting and no ad-hoc
      adjustment. Every score on this site is recomputable from its own inputs.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Composite range</th><th>What it means here</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Below the 3-signal gate</td><td>Parked, not scored yet</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scored, saturation or risk drags it down</td><td>Validate more / Park</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Strong opportunity</td><td>Clears the gate, scores well, real gap exists</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Build first experiment</td><td>The final call once evidence, saturation and risk all clear</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      One real signal, scored the way every analysis on this site is scored:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      "I sat there staring at my analytics dashboard — zero conversions, zero traffic, zero
      everything."
      <p>— IndieHackers, August 2026</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The account describes four months building in isolation (90% coding, 10% customer
      discovery), a launch with zero traffic, and a recovery only after switching to
      intent-based outreach in threads where buyers already gathered.
      <strong>Classification:</strong> a desperate, recurring problem rather than casual curiosity.
      It names a specific failure, a duration, and a concrete before and after.
      <strong>Independence:</strong> one first-person sufferer, one account, no advisor or meta
      framing. It counts as one signal toward the zero-audience distribution candidate, which
      reached 17 confirmed signals across five discovery runs before publication.
      <strong>What it does not prove on its own:</strong> that this one account's fix, targeted
      outreach over broadcasting, generalizes to every zero-audience founder. That claim rests on
      the pattern across all 17 signals. See the full analysis on
      <a href="/evidence/community-access-is-not-buyer-access/">community access vs. buyer access</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="See it applied"
    body="Read a full analysis built with this exact method, sources included."
    ctaHref="/evidence/reddit-product-validation/"
    ctaLabel="See a worked analysis"
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .section { padding-block: var(--s7) var(--s8); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--t-lede); color: var(--ink-soft); }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--t-body-s); margin: var(--s5) 0; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: var(--s3); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
  th { background: var(--surface-sunken); }
  blockquote {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--t-quote);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--hairline);
    padding-left: var(--s5);
    margin: var(--s6) 0;
  }
  blockquote p { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-style: normal; font-size: var(--t-caption); color: var(--muted); margin: var(--s2) 0 0; }
</style>
